# Milestone: v0.13.5 (LANG4-06)
# Covers: a `var` declared inside a block at chunk top should be scoped to
#         that block and must not clobber an outer chunk-top (realm-global)
#         binding of the same name; bare chunk-top vars keep realm-slot
#         persistence. Mirrors urbi's block-scoped var semantics (SDK 2.0
#         chapter 17).
# blocked: block-scoped chunk-top var un-masks a pre-existing unwind bug
#          (catch/unwind value corrupted by a frame-local var inside a
#          while-in-try) — see design-risks v0.13.5-B; LEDGERED per the
#          task-5 owner-pre-authorized bail arm.
# matrix-row: block-scoped-var
#
# Decision (2026-07-04, task-5 bail arm, owner-pre-authorized):
#   The minimal correct emit change is a one-condition gate in
#   emit_var_decl_arm (src/emit/uemit_expr.c): route the chunk-top global
#   install only for a BARE chunk-top var (fs->parent == NULL &&
#   fs->nblocks == 0), and let block-nested chunk-top vars fall through to
#   the existing frame-local else-branch.  That change makes cases 1-6
#   below pass (verified empirically) AND preserves bare-var REPL
#   persistence.
#
#   HOWEVER it regresses tests/chk/scheduler/dispatch_safepoint_pending_
#   unwind.chk by un-masking a PRE-EXISTING latent unwind bug: a frame-local
#   `var` declared inside a `while` loop that is inside a `try`, whose loop
#   body throws, corrupts the catch/unwind value delivery — the catch
#   variable binds to the realm-global object (`<object 0x...>`) instead of
#   the thrown value.  The bug is independent of block-scoped vars: it
#   reproduces identically inside a plain function body on the pre-change
#   binary (a code path the task-5 change never touches).  Only chunk-top
#   masked it, because block-nested chunk-top vars previously installed as
#   realm globals rather than frame-locals.
#
#   Repro (fails today inside a function body — no task-5 change involved):
#     (function () { var c = 0;
#        try { var i = 0; while (i < 1) { i = i + 1; throw 99 } }
#        catch (e) { c = e }; return c })()
#     -> <object 0x...>   (expected 99)
#   Removing EITHER the frame-local `var i` OR the `while` loop makes it
#   pass, isolating the combination.
#
#   Fixing that delivery path is the v0.13.1 capture/scoping/unwind seam the
#   task-5 BAIL-OUT RULE names, so the code change is REVERTED and this
#   fixture is filed as a specification record.  Activate once the unwind
#   catch/unwind-value bug (design-risks v0.13.5-B) is fixed; the emit gate
#   is then a one-line change.
#
# Original urbiscript (activate when v0.13.5-B lands):
# # --- 1. outer chunk-top var is unchanged after a shadowing block var
# # var x = 1; { var x = 2; }; x            -> [00000000] 1
# # --- 2. nested blocks each get their own scope; the outer stays 1
# # var y = 1; { var y = 2; { var y = 3; }; }; y   -> [00000001] 1
# # --- 3. block var with no initializer does not clobber the outer binding
# # var z = 1; { var z; }; z                -> [00000002] 1
# # --- 4. reading the outer chunk-top var from inside a block still works
# # var w = 5; { w }                        -> [00000003] 5
# # --- 5. assigning (no `var`) inside a block writes through to the outer var
# # var v = 1; { v = 2; }; v                -> [00000004] 2
# # --- 6. a closure inside a block captures the block-local var
# # var g = nil; { var q = 7; g = function () { return q }; }; g()  -> [00000005] 7
